WEBVTT

00:00:07.204 --> 00:00:10.648
Names and scores need the same shelf operations, but

00:00:10.648 --> 00:00:12.702
they use different value types.

00:00:12.702 --> 00:00:16.278
Generics allow us to write those operations once while

00:00:16.278 --> 00:00:18.729
the compiler checks each chosen type.

00:00:54.916 --> 00:00:58.556
T is a type parameter: a placeholder for a type, not a

00:00:58.556 --> 00:00:59.433
stored value.

00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:02.533
Sequence uses it for additions and retrievals.

00:01:02.533 --> 00:01:06.039
Shelf carries that same placeholder into its private

00:01:06.039 --> 00:01:09.679
ArrayList and implements each operation by calling the

00:01:09.679 --> 00:01:10.016
list.

00:01:10.339 --> 00:01:13.601
The names reference chooses String, while scores

00:01:13.601 --> 00:01:14.688
chooses Integer.

00:01:14.688 --> 00:01:16.591
Each shelf has its own list.

00:01:16.591 --> 00:01:19.988
The score retrievals provide Integer values, which

00:01:19.988 --> 00:01:21.891
Java unboxes for arithmetic.

00:01:21.891 --> 00:01:25.356
Predict the first name, name count, and total, then

00:01:25.356 --> 00:01:25.764
pause.

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:34.541
Index zero returns Maya, and the names shelf contains

00:01:34.541 --> 00:01:35.331
two entries.

00:01:35.331 --> 00:01:38.823
The score entries are four and seven, so their sum is

00:01:38.823 --> 00:01:39.284
eleven.

00:01:39.284 --> 00:01:42.315
The same methods work with either chosen type.

00:01:42.315 --> 00:01:45.543
The compiler would reject adding a number through

00:01:45.543 --> 00:01:48.574
names because that reference requires strings.

00:01:48.631 --> 00:01:50.860
Change the second score to zero.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.019
Predict all three output lines.

00:01:53.019 --> 00:01:56.361
Explain why the names output stays the same, and

00:01:56.361 --> 00:01:59.843
identify the type argument that controls the score

00:01:59.843 --> 00:02:00.331
values.
